\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Construction}
\title{A quadratic center and an independent trace observable}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{The [arithmetic family](fdip-000U) has both a quadratic form
and an operator relative to a specified pairing. To separate the
information they retain, consider rational data
##{\mathcal B=(V,g_0,q,m^2,V_{\mathrm{ret}},V_{\mathrm{el}}).}
Here #{V} has dimension four over #{\mathbb Q}, #{g_0} is a
rational positive definite symmetric pairing, and #{q} is a
rational [quadratic form](ca-0004) whose half-polar pairing
##{b_q(v,w)=\frac{q(v+w)-q(v)-q(w)}2}
is positive definite after extension to #{\mathbb R}.
Fix #{m^2\in\mathbb Q_{>0}} and a #{g_0}-orthogonal splitting
#{V=V_{\mathrm{ret}}\perp V_{\mathrm{el}}} into two-dimensional
summands. This is a field-level instance of the
[enriched background data](fdip-000G); the pairing, mass and
splitting are additional data, not consequences of #{q}.}

\p{The [relative precision](fdip-000Z) is the unique
operator #{Q:V\to V} satisfying
##{g_0(v,Qw)=b_q(v,w).}
It is rational, #{g_0}-self-adjoint and positive definite.
Define
##{\begin{aligned}
L_{\mathrm{rel}}&=Q-m^2I,\\
R(\mathcal B)&=\frac{\operatorname{tr}(L_{\mathrm{rel}}^2)}
 {\bigl(\operatorname{tr}L_{\mathrm{rel}}\bigr)^2}.
\end{aligned}}
Restrict these objects to #{L_{\mathrm{rel}}\geq0} and
#{\operatorname{tr}L_{\mathrm{rel}}\ne0}. This is the
[trace ratio](fdip-003H) on its stated domain. The real Gaussian
uses action #{q/2}, the volume induced by #{g_0}, and the
[normalized law](fdip-0005) #{\mu_Q}. Neither the factor #{1/2}
in the action nor the reference volume changes the convention
#{\iota_q(v)^2=q(v)1} of the [configuration Clifford algebra](fdip-000P).}

\p{An arrow #{T:\mathcal B\to\mathcal B'} is a rational
linear isomorphism preserving #{g_0,q,m^2} and carrying each
specified summand to its namesake. Preservation of the two
pairings gives
#{g'_0(Tv,Q'Tw)=g'_0(Tv,TQw)} for all #{v,w}, whence
##{Q'T=TQ,\qquad L'_{\mathrm{rel}}T=TL_{\mathrm{rel}}.}
Trace conjugacy proves #{R(\mathcal B')=R(\mathcal B)}.
Identity, inverse and composition preserve these conditions,
so the objects and arrows form a groupoid.}

\p{Forgetting the extra data leaves #{(V,q)}. Its
\vocabk{even Clifford algebra}{ca-001J} has quadratic center
##{E_q=Z(\operatorname{Cl}^+(q))
 \simeq\mathbb Q[z]/(z^2-d),}
where #{d} represents the signed discriminant class
#{\delta(q)\in\mathbb Q^\times /(\mathbb Q^\times)^2}.
In rank four this is the determinant class of #{b_q}.
This uses exactly the field/rank-four center statement and
proof of [the discriminant center](fcap-0043), without its
subsequent Spin identifications. In any common rational basis,
##{\det(b_q)=\det(g_0)\det(Q).}
Each pairing determinant changes by a square under a basis
change. Thus #{\det Q} alone does not supply #{\delta(q)}
unless the reference-pairing determinant class is also known.
TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.adjoinRootEquivCenterEven}
gives the generic center identification from a nonempty even
orthogonal spanning list of nonisotropic vectors over a field
of characteristic different from two. It supplies this algebraic
step, not the character or trace-observable construction.}

\p{Fix an algebraic closure #{\overline{\mathbb Q}}.
The associated quadratic Galois character is
##{\begin{aligned}
\chi_q:G_{\mathbb Q}&\longrightarrow\{1,-1\},\\
\sigma&\longmapsto\frac{\sigma(\sqrt d)}{\sqrt d},
\end{aligned}}
where #{G_{\mathbb Q}=\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)}.
The value is independent of the sign of the root and of
multiplying #{d} by a rational square. If #{d} is a square,
#{E_q\simeq\mathbb Q\times\mathbb Q} and #{\chi_q} is trivial.
Otherwise it is the character of #{\mathbb Q(\sqrt d)};
its cyclotomic description is
\citet{Section 1, p. 1}{conradQuadraticCharacters}.}

\p{The diagram records two constructions on enriched objects.
Its downward arrow is the independently defined trace ratio;
no arrow from #{\chi_q} to #{R} is asserted. A quadratic
isometry induces an even Clifford algebra isomorphism, hence a
center isomorphism and the same character.}
\tikzfigs{0.9}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
 every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
 arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}]
\node (b) at (0,0) {$\mathcal B$};
\node (q) at (2.2,0) {$(V,q)$};
\node (c) at (5,0) {$\operatorname{Cl}^+(q)$};
\node (e) at (5,-1.5) {$E_q$};
\node (x) at (2.2,-1.5) {$\chi_q$};
\node (r) at (0,-1.5) {$R(\mathcal B)\in\mathbb Q$};
\draw[arr] (b)--(q); \draw[arr] (q)--(c);
\draw[arr] (c)--(e); \draw[arr] (e)--(x);
\draw[arr] (b)--(r);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{For a nonsquare class, let #{d_0} be its squarefree integer
representative and put #{D=d_0} if #{d_0\equiv1\pmod4},
and #{D=4d_0} otherwise. The primitive Dirichlet representative
is #{\chi_D(n)=(D/n)}, with Kronecker-symbol convention and
value zero when #{\gcd(n,|D|)>1}; its conductor is #{|D|}.
For the split class use the trivial character of conductor one.
These conventions agree with
\citet{Sections 1 and 3, especially Theorem 3.4}{conradQuadraticCharacters}.
Fix the uncompleted convention
##{L(s,\chi_D)=\prod_{p\ \mathrm{prime}}
 (1-\chi_D(p)p^{-s})^{-1},\qquad\operatorname{Re}s>1,}
and the comparison point #{s=2}. The product converges absolutely;
no gamma or conductor factor is included. The Euler convention
is \citet{p. 5}{conradQuadraticCharacters}.
The [factorization criterion](fdip-0042) asks whether this
character retains enough information to recover #{R}.}
